window.docContents[28].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-characterization-of-the-automorphism-group":{"contents":"Converting the bijective homomorphism into a group isomorphism, and transporting it\nalong hypercubeIsoHamming (using Iso.autCongr from\nAutomorphism Groups), we obtain the\npromised characterization: the automorphism group of the n-dimensional hypercube\nis the hyperoctahedral group ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ.\n\n/-- The automorphism group of the Hamming graph is the hyperoctahedral group\nℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ. -/\nnoncomputable def hyperoctahedralMulEquivAutHamming (n : ℕ) :\n    hyperoctahedralGroup n ≃* (hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n) :=\n  MulEquiv.ofBijective (hyperoctahedralToAut n)\n    ⟨hyperoctahedralToAut_injective n, hyperoctahedralToAut_surjective n⟩\n\n\n/-- The automorphism group of the hypercube Q[n] is the hyperoctahedral group\nℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ. -/\nnoncomputable def autHypercubeMulEquivHyperoctahedral (n : ℕ) :\n    (Q[n] ≃g Q[n]) ≃* hyperoctahedralGroup n :=\n  (hypercubeIsoHamming n).autCongr.trans\n    (hyperoctahedralMulEquivAutHamming n).symm\n\n\nIn particular the hypercube Q[n] has exactly 2ⁿ · n! automorphisms.\n\nlemma card_aut_hypercube (n : ℕ) :\n    Nat.card (Q[n] ≃g Q[n]) = 2 ^ n * Nat.factorial n := by\n  rw [Nat.card_congr (autHypercubeMulEquivHyperoctahedral n).toEquiv,\n    Nat.card_congr (SemidirectProduct.equivProd), Nat.card_prod,\n    Nat.card_congr Multiplicative.toAdd]\n  simp [Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, Fintype.card_perm]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","header":"12.6.6. The Characterization of the Automorphism Group","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-characterization-of-the-automorphism-group"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-support":{"contents":"The support of a graph is the set of vertices that belong to at least one edge.\n\n#check mem_support -- (G : SimpleGraph V) {v : V} : v ∈ G.support ↔ ∃ w, G.Adj v w\n\n\nIf G.Adj u v then u is in the support of G.\n\nexample (h : G.Adj u v) : u ∈ G.support := h.left_mem_support\n\n\nNotice that in the previous example we could use h.left_mem_support where\nh : G.Adj u v since the theorem we applied is in the SimpleGraph.Adj namespace.\n\n#check SimpleGraph.Adj.left_mem_support\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph\u0009Vertices","header":"2.3.1. Support","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-support"}});